@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Conversion Functions";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            SQL Server Conversion Functions
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            Conversion Functions in SQL Server are used to convert
            a value from one data type to another data type.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What are Conversion Functions?
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server stores different types of data such as
            integers, strings, decimal values and dates.
            Sometimes we need to convert one data type into another
            data type.
        </p>

        <p>
            SQL Server provides conversion functions such as
            <strong>CAST()</strong>, <strong>CONVERT()</strong> and
            <strong>TRY_CAST()</strong> to perform these conversions.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">

            <strong>Example:</strong>

            <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                Suppose the value <strong>'100'</strong> is stored
                as text. If we want to perform mathematical operations
                on it, we can convert it into an integer.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         COMMON FUNCTIONS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common Conversion Functions
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Function</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>CAST()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Converts an expression from one data type
                            to another.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>CAST('100' AS INT)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>CONVERT()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Converts an expression into another data type
                            and supports style formatting.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>CONVERT(INT, '100')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>TRY_CAST()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Attempts to convert a value and returns NULL
                            if conversion fails.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>TRY_CAST('ABC' AS INT)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>TRY_CONVERT()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Attempts conversion and returns NULL when
                            conversion is unsuccessful.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>TRY_CONVERT(INT, 'ABC')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         1. CAST()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            1. CAST() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>CAST()</strong> function is used to convert
            an expression from one data type to another data type.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CAST(expression AS data_type)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example 1: VARCHAR to INT
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CAST('100' AS INT);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 100
        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example 2: INT to VARCHAR
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CAST(500 AS VARCHAR(10));</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 500
        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example 3: DECIMAL to INT
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CAST(125.75 AS INT);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 125
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         2. CONVERT()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            2. CONVERT() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>CONVERT()</strong> function is also used
            to convert one data type into another.
        </p>

        <p>
            One important feature of CONVERT() is that it can use
            a <strong>style</strong> parameter for formatting
            date and time values.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CONVERT(data_type, expression)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CONVERT(INT, '250');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 250
        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Date Conversion Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CONVERT(VARCHAR(10), GETDATE(), 103);</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-3">
            Style <strong>103</strong> formats the date in
            <strong>dd/mm/yyyy</strong> format.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         3. TRY_CAST()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            3. TRY_CAST() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>TRY_CAST()</strong> function attempts to
            convert a value into the specified data type.
        </p>

        <p>
            If the conversion is successful, the converted value
            is returned. If the conversion fails, SQL Server returns
            <strong>NULL</strong> instead of raising a conversion error.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>TRY_CAST(expression AS data_type)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Successful Conversion
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT TRY_CAST('500' AS INT);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 500
        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Failed Conversion
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT TRY_CAST('ABC' AS INT);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> NULL
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         4. TRY_CONVERT()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            4. TRY_CONVERT() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>TRY_CONVERT()</strong> function attempts
            to convert an expression into the specified data type.
        </p>

        <p>
            If conversion cannot be performed, it returns
            <strong>NULL</strong>.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>TRY_CONVERT(data_type, expression)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT TRY_CONVERT(INT, 'ABC');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> NULL
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         CAST VS CONVERT
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            CAST() vs CONVERT()
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>CAST()</th>
                        <th>CONVERT()</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            Standard SQL conversion function.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            SQL Server-specific conversion function.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            Syntax is simple and readable.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Supports style formatting.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <code>CAST(value AS INT)</code>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>CONVERT(INT, value)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         PRACTICAL TABLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example with Student Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we have a Students table where Marks are stored
            as VARCHAR values.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Marks VARCHAR(10)
);</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Insert Sample Data
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES
(1, 'Rahul', '85'),
(2, 'Priya', '92'),
(3, 'Amit', '76');</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Convert Marks into INT
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    StudentName,
    Marks,
    CAST(Marks AS INT) AS NumericMarks
FROM Students;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Calculate Total Marks
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    SUM(CAST(Marks AS INT)) AS TotalMarks
FROM Students;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Calculate Average Marks
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    AVG(CAST(Marks AS INT)) AS AverageMarks
FROM Students;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         PRACTICAL DATE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Date Conversion
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can convert a date into a formatted string using
            CONVERT().
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CONVERT(VARCHAR(10), GETDATE(), 103)
AS FormattedDate;</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">

            <strong>Example format:</strong>

            <br />

            05/10/2026

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHEN TO USE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            When Do We Use Conversion Functions?
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When numeric data is stored as text.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When text needs to be converted into numeric values.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When dates need to be displayed in a particular format.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When data from different sources has different data types.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When performing calculations on converted values.
            </li>

            <li>
                When invalid conversion should return NULL instead
                of causing an error.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning">

            <h4 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    CAST() converts an expression to another data type.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    CONVERT() also converts data types and supports
                    style formatting.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    TRY_CAST() returns NULL when conversion fails.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    TRY_CONVERT() returns NULL when conversion fails.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Conversion is useful when different data types
                    need to work together.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Always make sure that the source value can be
                    safely converted to the target data type.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-primary">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                SQL Server Conversion Functions are used to convert
                values from one data type to another. In this topic,
                we learned CAST(), CONVERT(), TRY_CAST() and
                TRY_CONVERT(). These functions are very useful when
                working with numeric values, text values, dates and
                data received from different sources.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


